1. Introduction to Applications
Understanding the differences between desktop applications and web applications.
Examples of desktop applications: Microsoft Word, VLC Media Player, and Visual Studio Code.
Examples of web applications: Gmail, Google Docs, and Instagram Web.
2. Understanding Web Application Architecture
Frontend: The user interface that users see and interact with.
Backend: Processes requests and handles application logic.
Database: Stores and manages application data.
Client–Server Communication: Enables the frontend and backend to exchange information.
I also learned about the three tiers of web application architecture: Presentation Tier, Application Tier, and Data Tier.
3. Introduction to Frontend Technologies
HTML: Structures webpage content.
CSS: Styles webpages and improves their appearance.
Bootstrap: Helps build responsive websites.
JavaScript: Adds interactivity and dynamic behavior.
React.js: Helps build interactive and reusable user interfaces.
I was also introduced to Vibe Coding, an AI-assisted approach to software development using natural language prompts.
4. Getting Started with HTML
I started learning HTML (HyperText Markup Language), the standard markup language used to structure webpages.
Topics covered:
Basic structure of an HTML document.
HTML tags, elements, and attributes.
Headings, paragraphs, and horizontal rules.
Text formatting using bold, italic, and underline.
The purpose of the <head> and <body> sections.
Creating basic webpage content and hyperlinks.
5. History and Evolution of HTML
I learned about Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web and a key contributor to the development of HTML.
I also explored HTML5 features, including semantic elements, audio and video support, canvas graphics, and modern form controls.
This is the beginning of another exciting phase in my learning journey! I look forward to strengthening my frontend development skills, practicing consistently, and building projects by applying these concepts.